From 415483ff9784c38a88b273363a7efd0c227b95ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:11:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] custody v2: persist-before-ack barrier for member shares,
 approvals and acks

Root's F1, confirmed on c635fd68: the daemon's member store fired its sealed-state write and returned undefined, and the
member submitted createCommittee or ackHandover without waiting, so the chain could count a share the host never stored.

- sealed-state.mjs: memberStore.save RETURNS the write (logged and rethrown). The serialized write order is unchanged.
  The host-policy store is deliberately unchanged: a newly anchored ban must apply at once; its record only carries
  the anti-rollback high-water across restarts.
- member.mjs commit(): applies a change, awaits the write, and on failure restores the in-memory state IN PLACE (the
  daemon's state.member is the same object), so no later write, retry or restart acts on a change that never became
  durable. Commits run one at a time.
  - Awaited before: createCommittee (genesis share), ackHandover (pending share), returning an approval signature or
    an adopted independent share, and any tick that deals or proposes.
  - Also used for host binding, candidate records and the epoch activation/erasure.
- handover retry safety: the recipient records the new commitments it computed and whether its ack went out.
  - A failed ack is re-sent from the SAME durable share.
  - A share left from an earlier (expired) proposal for the epoch is durably replaced before the new ack.
  - The previous "pending exists -> skip" never re-acked.

test/custody-v2-persist-barrier.test.mjs: 10 tests through the real member and the real writer. A held, completed or
failed host disk records what was durable at every submission. The commit-1 tree fails 9 (the counterexample); this
commit passes 10. W1, serialization, passes on both.
